# No dukpy cheat — native HTML/JS/CSS CPU

This product is a **real JavaScript-native computer** (HTML5 Canvas games,
minimal CSS as needed) destined for **FPGA then ASIC** — same ladder as
`JMR-BASIC-FPGA-COMPUTER`, not a browser wrapped around a soft CPU.

## Hard cheats (forbidden)

- **dukpy / Duktape / V8 / QuickJS / Node / browser** executing game logic
  as the PYTHON / FPGA-SIM / BOARD / ASIC “machine.”
- Silent **host twin** for F9 FPGA-SIM (`JMR_SIM_HOST=1` is opt-in debug only;
  never default — see `never-fake-fpga-sim`).
- Claiming BOARD/ASIC done because Chrome or dukpy looked good.
- Shipping **same-name twin titles** (`INVADERS.JS` vs `INVADERS.HTML`) as if
  they were the product — **one title = one `NAME.HTML`**.

## Honest path (required)

```
LOAD "NAME.HTML"  →  edit (line numbers = HTML)  →  RUN
    →  always compile current HTML  →  in-memory ProgramImage  →  JMR VM
    →  full-quality graphics → ASET section → external SRAM asset bank,
       never code BRAM (no NAME.DAT — that design is retired)
```

| Runtime | What runs the game |
|---|---|
| Chrome | Browser (authoring / visual check only — not the machine) |
| PYTHON | **Bytecode VM** — **compile-on-RUN** from loaded HTML |
| FPGA-SIM | **Verilator RTL** of that VM (same UX; compile current HTML) |
| BOARD / ASIC | **Same RTL ISA** on silicon |

Order: **PYTHON bytecode perfect → FPGA-SIM perfect → board → ASIC.**
Never skip; never fake a rung.

## Compile-on-RUN (hard rule)

- **Source of truth = the loaded `.HTML`** (editor buffer).
- **`RUN` always recompiles** that HTML → an in-memory ProgramImage.
  Compile errors must show **line numbers from the current HTML**.
- **Do not write or LOAD a `.JSH` file.** Compile output is ephemeral
  (code + ASET in the ProgramImage). Card and `storage/` are HTML only.
- FPGA-SIM/BOARD stream that ProgramImage into the VM / BRAM on RUN.
  Missing compile path → fail loud (`?NH` debt), never Invaders hex.

## Asset bank (hard rule — external SRAM, replaces the old `.DAT` spill)

Great graphics must survive at **full quality**. Do **not** shrink or drop
sprite sheets to “make it fit.” Donkey is the example: the HTML is huge
because of `data:image` PNGs, not because JS needs megabytes of RAM.

- **HTML stays the authoring file** (Chrome `data:` URIs OK). User never
  splits art by hand.
- **`RUN` extracts** `data:image` / fat literals at full resolution into the
  **ASET section** of the ProgramImage (per-title 256-entry palette +
  8-bpp indexed banks). The loader streams ASET into the **external 4 MB
  SRAM asset bank** (2M × 16 IS61WV204816 contract; FPGA board bridges DDR3
  behind the same simple SRAM port; FPGA-SIM uses a behavioral model behind
  the identical port).
- Bytecode stores **handles/descriptors only** (w, h, SRAM offset).
  `drawImage` blits straight from asset SRAM. Pixels never enter code BRAM.
- **No `NAME.DAT` file exists.** The ProgramImage carries code + ASET in
  memory. EDIT HTML then RUN recompiles; there is no sidecar to prefer.
- **Forbidden:** packing Donkey art into code BRAM; downscaling sheets to
  squeeze into code BRAM (`180000` pixel cap trap); quantizing to 8 colors.
- **BRAM = fast on-chip RAM** (dual FB + code + heap — ASIC target is
  ~30 mm², custom padring, ~1 MB-class on-chip SRAM; do not shrink BRAM to
  fit a small die). **µSD = disk** (`NAME.HTML`). **External SRAM =
  asset bank.** Do not fake a 64K BASIC house.

## Files

- **Edit / LOAD / play:** `storage/*.HTML` only (`LOAD "NAME.HTML"` / `RUN`).
- **No `.JSH` files.** Compile-on-RUN stays in memory. No separate graphics file.
- **`storage/games_*`:** upstream archive only — not DIR, not card, not twins.

## Agent checklist before marking HTML “done”

PYTHON and FPGA-SIM must **look and play the same** (full game). Board is
later, after the user F9-approves.

1. Same `NAME.HTML` works in Chrome (authoring look/feel check only).
2. PYTHON `LOAD`+`RUN` = **compile-on-RUN bytecode**, not dukpy.
   Donkey-class art lands full quality in the external SRAM asset bank
   (ASET section; never packed into or downscaled for code BRAM).
3. FPGA-SIM F9 shows **RTL**, same LOAD+RUN, **same glass/keys/graphics**.
4. Do **not** rewrite the three HTML titles down to a toy subset to “pass.”
5. Board confirms only after PYTHON + FPGA-SIM match and the user approves.
